VERDICT
Because your code first rules out zero and negative numbers, then strips away all allowed prime factors by division, leaving exactly 1 if no other prime factors exist, your solution is entirely correct.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: You handled the negative and zero edge cases right at the start so the loops do not run forever. You also used clean, simple loops to divide out the factors.

Is it correct? Yes, it is fully correct and passes all LeetCode test cases.

Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: There are no logical bugs. You perfectly handled the edge case of 1, which has no prime factors, so it skips all loops and correctly returns true since 1 equals 1. You also safely handle huge prime numbers, which instantly skip the loops and return false without wasting time.

Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason: Time complexity is O(log n) because dividing the number repeatedly by 2, 3, or 5 drastically shrinks its size in very few steps. Space complexity is O(1) because you only modify the input variable directly and do not use any extra memory.

Why my approach works, or why it doesn't: An ugly number is like a smoothie made of only three allowed ingredients: 2, 3, and 5. By dividing those exact numbers out over and over, you filter out all the ingredients. If you end up at exactly 1, it means there were no hidden forbidden ingredients left behind.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Recursive Division

Where it comes from: Common functional programming approach seen in LeetCode discussions.

Idea: Instead of loops, we can use a helper function to divide the number by 2, 3, or 5 and call the function again. If we reach 1, it is true. If we reach a number that cannot be divided by any of them, it is false.

C++ code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    bool isUgly(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) return false;
        if (n == 1) return true;
        if (n % 2 == 0) return isUgly(n / 2);
        if (n % 3 == 0) return isUgly(n / 3);
        if (n % 5 == 0) return isUgly(n / 5);
        return false;
    }
};
Time and space complexity: Time is O(log n) for the divisions. Space is O(log n) because every function call takes up memory on the computer's call stack.

What makes the next approach better: Recursion wastes memory. Using standard while loops takes up no extra memory.

Approach 2: Iterative Division (Your Approach - Optimal)

Where it comes from: The official LeetCode editorial and standard math textbooks.

Idea: Keep dividing the number by 2, 3, and 5 as long as there is no remainder. Check if the final result is 1.

