Time and space complexity: Time is O(log n) and Space is O(1).

What makes the next approach better: This is the most optimal approach. We can only make it slightly more flexible by grouping the loops into an array if we had many allowed prime factors, but for just three, this is perfect.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use? You used Approach 2, the iterative division method.

Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem? They expect exactly your approach because it solves the problem with the best possible time and space limits without overcomplicating things.

Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them: Question: How would you change this if the allowed prime factors were given in a list instead of just 2, 3, and 5? Answer: I would loop through that list of prime numbers. For each prime in the list, I would run a while loop to divide the number by that prime as long as the remainder is 0.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

The key insight, in one sentence: If a number is only made of specific prime factors, dividing those factors out completely will always leave exactly 1.

